guarding the old \"tree of the world\", is reinterpreted by Vlachopoulos as a 
scorpion, a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography. The 
snippet notes that the ring measures 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and is the fifth‑largest
Aegean signet ring. The paper therefore provides precise physical dimensions, a 
refined iconographic reading of the scene (including the scorpion, sacred tree, 
and associated figures), arguments supporting an authentic Late Bronze Age date,
and discussion of the ring’s significance within Bronze Age religious symbolism.
The PDF hosted on Academia.edu offers full text access, but the chapter is part 
of a scholarly volume and thus a suitable academic source with stable 
bibliographic details for citation."
  }
]
