Blakolmer (ed.), Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography 
(Aegis 18, pp. 223‑252). Vlachopoulos presents the results of extended research 
documenting the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor and reassessing cases where 
Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings have been misidentified as fakes. He explains that 
suspicions about the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos 
were fueled by obscure and legendary discovery circumstances but argues that 
recent scholarship tends to accept them as genuine. This revised view is 
supported by newly discovered comparanda with similar iconographic subjects 
(wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories) and by detailed study of the 
techniques and craftsmanship of unique signet rings from the early Late Bronze 
Age (16th–15th century BC). The chapter provides precise physical dimensions of 
the Ring of Nestor (3.29 cm by 2.18 cm), noting it as the fifth-largest Aegean 
signet ring, and offers a refined iconographic interpretation of the key motif: 
a creature described by Evans as a \"watch‑dog\" or \"dragon\" guarding an old 
