"# Ring of Nestor\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is a large Aegean Bronze 
Age gold signet ring, now in the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, whose engraved bezel 
shows a complex sequence of ritual scenes that have made it a key object in the 
study of Minoan and Mycenaean religion and art. The ring is part of a funerary 
treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals said to come from a burial at 
Thisbê in Boeotia, central Greece, and became widely known through Arthur J. 
Evans’s 1925 study in the Journal of Hellenic Studies. On stylistic and 
technical grounds it is generally dated to the early Late Bronze Age, in the 
Late Minoan I period around the 16th–15th centuries BC. Scholars have emphasized
its unusual size, detailed gold engraving, and multi‑scene iconography, which 
includes a sacred tree, altars, human or divine figures, and a central guardian 
creature variously interpreted as a watch‑dog, dragon, or scorpion. The ring’s 
name alludes to the Homeric hero Nestor, reflecting early attempts to connect 
its imagery with Homeric conceptions of the afterworld, while more recent work 
has focused on its place within Aegean glyptic art and ongoing debates about its
authenticity and meaning. [4][5][6][8][10][9]\n\n## Conflicting interpretations 
of the guardian creature\n\nA notable point of disagreement in the 
interpretation of the Ring of Nestor concerns the nature of the guardian figure 
adjacent to the sacred tree. Evans described this creature as a watch‑dog or 
dragon that guards what he called the \"tree of the world\", integrating it into
a broader reading of the scene as a vision of the Minoan after‑world. In 
contrast, Vlachopoulos argues that the figure should be identified as a 
scorpion, drawing on parallels where scorpions appear as major apotropaic 
symbols in Bronze Age Aegean iconography. This divergence reflects different 
approaches to the ring’s imagery, with Evans leaning on mythological analogies 
and Vlachopoulos emphasizing iconographic and comparative criteria. 
